  "body": "The HTML spec [says:](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/scripting.html#custom-element)\n\n> After a custom element is created, changing the value of the is attribute does not change the element's behaviour.\n\nI think this could be a bit clearer by making it a requirement, and specifying what is meant by &ldquo;behavior&rdquo;. Does this mean that the is attribute value is cached at element creation? The old W3C spec did it this way explicitly. There are some spec problems with this: First, parsed elements are created in DOM without their attributes. Second, the element can be created well before it is upgraded, and [define](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/scripting.html#dom-customelementsregistry-define) step 21 implies dynamism&mdash;there&rsquo;s a census of the is attribute at that point in time. (Neither of these are problems for our implementation.)\n\nOn the other hand, if the intent is to make is attribute handling more dynamic, the prose quoted above is inaccurate. It should say that changing is does not change the elements behavior after (insert some time referring to whenever the is attribute is tested.)\n",
